# ZZZKidz Project Standards

These rules govern every change in this repository.

The goal is to build a premium, highly responsive, high-performance, SEO-ready, media-rich website that is easy to maintain, easy to market digitally, secure by default, and production-ready on Vercel.

Prioritize:

1. User experience
2. Mobile responsiveness
3. Performance
4. SEO and digital marketing
5. Accessibility
6. Security
7. Reusability and maintainability
8. Visual polish
9. Reliability
10. Simplicity

Never sacrifice these priorities for speed of implementation.

## Rule Compliance Reporting

Before and after implementing changes, check against this rule book.

If any proposed or completed change conflicts with these standards:

- Call it out clearly to the user
- Name the violated rule section(s)
- Explain the conflict briefly
- Prefer a compliant alternative unless the user explicitly overrides

Do not silently ship work that violates non-negotiable principles.

---

# 1. General Engineering Principles

- Write production-quality code.
- Keep code simple, readable, predictable, and maintainable.
- Prefer established project patterns over introducing new patterns.
- Make the smallest appropriate change for the requested task.
- Do not refactor unrelated code unless necessary to safely complete the task.
- Do not introduce unnecessary abstractions.
- Do not duplicate logic that can reasonably be shared.
- Prefer explicit, understandable code over clever code.
- Avoid premature optimization, but never knowingly introduce avoidable performance problems.
- Do not create technical debt unnecessarily.
- Preserve existing functionality unless the task explicitly requires changing it.
- Never silently change user-facing behaviour.
- When a meaningful architectural decision is required, choose the simplest scalable approach.

---

# 2. Architecture and Code Reuse

Code reuse is a core project requirement.

- Reuse existing components, utilities, hooks, layouts, styles, and services before creating new ones.
- Search the existing codebase before creating a new component or utility.
- If a behaviour is used in multiple places, consider making it reusable.
- Global behaviours must be implemented once and reused wherever possible.
- Shared visual patterns must use shared components rather than duplicated markup.
- Shared business logic must live in reusable utilities, services, hooks, or modules.
- Do not copy and paste large blocks of code between pages.
- Avoid page-specific implementations when a reusable solution is practical.
- Prefer composition over unnecessary inheritance.
- Keep components focused and reusable.
- Avoid components becoming massive monolithic files.
- Separate presentation, business logic, data fetching, and reusable utilities where appropriate.
- Maintain a clear folder structure.
- Do not create duplicate versions of the same component with slightly different names unless there is a real functional difference.
- Before introducing a new abstraction, confirm that an existing abstraction cannot be extended.

## Global Design Behaviour

Implement recurring behaviours centrally where practical:

- Typography
- Spacing
- Containers
- Buttons
- Forms
- Cards
- Navigation
- Responsive breakpoints
- Shadows
- Borders
- Radius
- Animations
- Transitions
- Loading states
- Error states
- Focus states
- Section layouts
- Media handling

Global behaviour must not be recreated independently on every page.

---

# 3. Technology and Deployment

The production application is intended to run on:

- Next.js
- TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS or the existing project styling system
- Vercel for deployment
- GoDaddy for domain registration/DNS where applicable

Rules:

- Build in a manner compatible with Vercel production deployment.
- Do not introduce server/runtime dependencies that are incompatible with Vercel without a strong reason.
- Do not hardcode development URLs.
- Production URLs must come from configuration/environment variables where appropriate.
- Never assume localhost in production.
- Production must use HTTPS.
- Do not hardcode domain-specific assumptions into reusable components.
- Ensure server/client boundaries are intentional.
- Prefer server-side rendering/static generation where it provides SEO or performance benefits.
- Use client-side JavaScript only where necessary.

---

# 4. Mobile-First Responsive Design

Mobile responsiveness is NON-NEGOTIABLE.

The website must work correctly across:

- Small mobile phones
- Large mobile phones
- Small tablets
- Large tablets
- Small laptops
- Standard laptops
- Large desktop monitors
- Very wide screens

There must be no:

- Horizontal scrolling
- Clipped content
- Overlapping elements
- Broken grids
- Distorted images
- Broken navigation
- Overflowing text
- Inaccessible buttons
- Misaligned sections
- Unusable forms
- Unsymmetrical layouts
- Unexpected empty space
- Content hidden accidentally at certain widths

Rules:

- Build mobile-first.
- Enhance progressively for larger screens.
- Do not design desktop first and merely shrink it for mobile.
- Use responsive layouts rather than fixed-position layouts wherever possible.
- Avoid hardcoded widths unless genuinely required.
- Use fluid sizing where appropriate.
- Use responsive typography.
- Use responsive spacing.
- Use responsive images.
- Use responsive grids/flex layouts.
- Ensure navigation works properly on touch devices.
- Ensure buttons and interactive elements are easy to tap.
- Avoid hover-only functionality.
- Do not rely on mouse interactions for critical functionality.
- Test at multiple viewport widths before considering a task complete.
- Consider extremely narrow screens as well as large displays.
- Ensure content remains visually balanced at every breakpoint.

Never fix a responsive problem by hiding meaningful content unless explicitly approved.

---

# 5. Visual Design: Premium and Glossy

The website must feel:

- Premium
- Modern
- Polished
- Energetic
- Trustworthy
- Professional
- Visually refined

Avoid:

- Generic template appearance
- Excessive gradients
- Excessive rounded cards
- Random shadows
- Excessive animations
- Cheap-looking UI effects
- Inconsistent spacing
- Inconsistent typography
- Visually noisy layouts
- Excessive decorative elements
- Unnecessary glassmorphism
- Overly flashy interactions

Use:

- Strong visual hierarchy
- Consistent spacing
- Intentional typography
- High-quality imagery
- Sophisticated contrast
- Consistent border/radius/shadow treatment
- Subtle depth
- Controlled animation
- Strong composition
- Generous whitespace where appropriate

Premium does not mean excessive.

Every visual effect must have a purpose.

---

# 6. Design System

Use a consistent design system throughout the application.

Centralize where practical:

- Colors
- Typography
- Font sizes
- Font weights
- Spacing
- Breakpoints
- Border radius
- Shadows
- Container widths
- Button styles
- Form styles
- Animation timings
- Z-index conventions

Do not introduce arbitrary values repeatedly when a design token can be used.

Avoid visual drift between pages.

A button should look like the same product wherever it appears.

A heading hierarchy should remain consistent throughout the site.

---

# 7. Media Optimization

This website is media-heavy. Media performance is therefore a core engineering requirement.

Images and videos must:

- Load reliably
- Render correctly
- Maintain aspect ratio
- Avoid layout shifts
- Look sharp without excessive file size
- Work correctly on mobile and desktop
- Not make the page feel slow or "sticky"

Rules:

- Use modern image formats where supported.
- Use responsive image sizing.
- Serve appropriately sized images rather than unnecessarily large originals.
- Lazy-load non-critical media.
- Prioritize above-the-fold media appropriately.
- Define image dimensions or aspect ratios before loading.
- Prevent cumulative layout shift.
- Use optimized thumbnails/posters for videos.
- Do not load full-resolution media when a smaller version is sufficient.
- Do not embed unnecessarily heavy media.
- Use CDN-backed media delivery where practical.
- Prefer optimized media services such as Cloudinary or equivalent where appropriate.
- Do not load every image on a page immediately unless justified.
- Avoid large background images when a better implementation exists.

## Video Rules

- Do not autoplay heavy videos unless there is a clear UX reason.
- Use poster images for videos.
- Use appropriate preload behaviour.
- Do not load multiple large videos simultaneously without necessity.
- Ensure videos have correct aspect ratios.
- Ensure controls are usable on mobile.
- Ensure videos do not overflow containers.
- Provide graceful fallback behaviour if media fails.
- Do not let media block page usability.

Never use a giant image/video simply because the source asset is available.

---

# 8. Performance

Performance is a first-class feature.

Optimize for:

- Core Web Vitals
- Largest Contentful Paint
- Cumulative Layout Shift
- Interaction to Next Paint
- Time to First Byte
- Total page weight
- JavaScript execution
- Image delivery
- Font loading

Rules:

- Minimize unnecessary JavaScript.
- Avoid unnecessary client components.
- Prefer server-rendered content where practical.
- Avoid large dependencies for small functionality.
- Reuse existing dependencies before adding new ones.
- Avoid expensive rendering logic.
- Avoid unnecessary API requests.
- Cache data where appropriate.
- Avoid rendering hidden heavy components unnecessarily.
- Defer non-critical functionality.
- Lazy-load non-critical media and components where appropriate.
- Avoid unnecessary third-party scripts.
- Third-party marketing scripts must be added intentionally and efficiently.

Do not optimize by sacrificing usability, accessibility, SEO, or visual quality.

---

# 9. SEO

SEO is NON-NEGOTIABLE.

Every publicly accessible page must be intentionally optimized for search engines.

Rules:

- Every page must have a unique title.
- Every page must have a unique meta description.
- Use canonical URLs where appropriate.
- Use Open Graph metadata.
- Use appropriate social sharing metadata.
- Use semantic HTML.
- Maintain logical heading hierarchy.
- Use descriptive URLs.
- Use descriptive internal links.
- Use descriptive anchor text.
- Avoid duplicate content where possible.
- Generate/update sitemap functionality.
- Provide robots.txt functionality.
- Ensure important pages are crawlable.
- Do not accidentally block search engines.
- Do not expose staging/test URLs as canonical URLs.
- Use structured data/schema markup where appropriate.
- Ensure structured data matches visible page content.
- Optimize image filenames and alt text appropriately.
- Avoid keyword stuffing.
- Do not generate meaningless SEO text solely for search engines.

Important SEO content should be rendered in crawlable HTML rather than relying exclusively on client-side JavaScript.

---

# 10. Digital Marketing

The website must be built as a marketing platform, not merely a brochure.

Every important page should support conversion.

Include infrastructure for:

- Contact forms
- Lead capture
- Enquiry forms
- Strong calls-to-action
- Social sharing
- Campaign landing pages
- Search traffic
- Paid advertising traffic
- Analytics
- Conversion tracking
- Attribution
- Remarketing where legally appropriate

Important CTAs should be:

- Clearly visible
- Consistent
- Mobile-friendly
- Action-oriented
- Trackable

Do not create pages without considering their marketing purpose.

Every major page should have a clear intended action.

Examples:

- Enquire
- Book a demo
- Contact us
- Request information
- Talk to the team
- Register interest

---

# 11. Analytics and Tracking

The website must support appropriate analytics and marketing tracking.

Potential integrations may include:

- Google Analytics
- Google Search Console
- Google Tag Manager
- Meta Pixel
- Other approved advertising/conversion platforms

Rules:

- Tracking must be intentional and documented.
- Track important conversions.
- Track CTA interactions where useful.
- Track form submissions.
- Avoid duplicate events.
- Do not fire events multiple times accidentally.
- Do not expose tracking secrets client-side.
- Do not hardcode private API credentials.
- Respect applicable privacy/consent requirements.
- Marketing scripts must not significantly degrade performance.

Do not remove existing analytics/tracking without explicit approval.

---

# 12. Accessibility

Build to a strong WCAG-aligned standard.

Rules:

- Use semantic HTML.
- Provide meaningful alt text for informative images.
- Decorative images should be appropriately marked.
- Maintain logical heading hierarchy.
- Ensure sufficient contrast.
- Ensure keyboard navigation works.
- Provide visible focus states.
- Do not rely exclusively on color.
- Forms must have proper labels.
- Interactive elements must have accessible names.
- Modals/dialogues must be keyboard accessible.
- Mobile interactions must remain accessible.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences where practical.
- Avoid inaccessible custom controls when native HTML can solve the problem.

Accessibility must not be treated as an afterthought.

---

# 13. Security and Secrets

Security is NON-NEGOTIABLE.

NEVER expose or commit:

- API keys
- API secrets
- Database credentials
- Passwords
- Private tokens
- Authentication secrets
- Service account credentials
- Encryption keys
- Webhook secrets
- Private environment variables
- Internal infrastructure credentials

Rules:

- Use environment variables for secrets.
- Never commit `.env` files containing secrets.
- Maintain an appropriate `.gitignore`.
- Use public environment variables only when the value is genuinely safe to expose.
- Assume all client-side code is public.
- Never place secrets in frontend JavaScript.
- Never expose privileged API credentials in browser requests.
- Validate and sanitize untrusted input.
- Protect server-side endpoints appropriately.
- Avoid leaking internal errors to users.
- Do not expose stack traces in production.
- Do not expose internal database structure unnecessarily.
- Do not log secrets.
- Do not include secrets in URLs.
- Do not hardcode credentials in source files.
- Do not copy secrets into documentation or examples.

If a secret appears in code, stop and fix the security issue rather than continuing.

---

# 14. Environment Variables

Use environment variables appropriately.

Rules:

- Separate development and production configuration where necessary.
- Never commit actual production secrets.
- Clearly distinguish public and server-only environment variables.
- Variables intended for browser exposure must contain only non-sensitive information.
- Document required environment variables without documenting their secret values.
- Fail safely when required configuration is missing.
- Never print environment variables for debugging in production.

---

# 15. Forms and User Input

Forms are conversion-critical and must be robust.

Rules:

- Validate input on the client for UX.
- Validate input again on the server for security.
- Provide clear validation messages.
- Prevent duplicate submissions.
- Provide loading states.
- Provide success states.
- Provide failure states.
- Preserve user-entered data where appropriate after validation failure.
- Make forms fully usable on mobile.
- Use accessible labels and error messages.
- Protect forms from obvious abuse/spam where appropriate.
- Track successful conversions where analytics is configured.

Never assume client-side validation is sufficient for security.

---

# 16. Error Handling

Every production feature must have sensible failure behaviour.

Rules:

- Handle network failures.
- Handle missing data.
- Handle invalid input.
- Handle failed media loading.
- Handle API failures.
- Provide meaningful user-facing messages.
- Avoid exposing technical implementation details.
- Never leave users with a broken-looking interface.
- Use appropriate loading, empty, success, and error states.

---

# 17. Loading States

Loading states must feel intentional and premium.

Use appropriate:

- Skeletons
- Spinners
- Placeholders
- Progressive loading
- Blur-up techniques where appropriate

Rules:

- Avoid content jumping during load.
- Do not display unnecessary loading indicators for extremely short operations.
- Do not block the entire page unnecessarily.
- Preserve layout dimensions where possible.

---

# 18. Animation and Interaction

Animations should enhance the experience rather than distract from content.

Rules:

- Prefer subtle, polished animation.
- Keep transitions consistent.
- Avoid excessive motion.
- Avoid animation that delays access to content.
- Avoid animation that harms performance.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences.
- Do not animate every element simply because animation is possible.
- Avoid parallax unless justified.
- Avoid scroll-jacking.
- Avoid interaction patterns that feel gimmicky.

---

# 19. Typography

Typography must remain readable and responsive.

Rules:

- Use a controlled typography system.
- Avoid excessive font families.
- Optimize font loading.
- Ensure text remains readable on small screens.
- Prevent unexpected text overflow.
- Maintain consistent heading hierarchy.
- Use appropriate line heights.
- Avoid walls of text.
- Ensure important marketing copy remains visually prominent.

---

# 20. Navigation

Navigation must be intuitive and consistent.

Rules:

- Navigation must work on mobile and desktop.
- Mobile navigation must be touch-friendly.
- Critical pages must remain easily accessible.
- Avoid excessively deep navigation structures.
- Maintain consistent header/footer behaviour.
- Ensure active states are clear.
- Ensure keyboard accessibility.
- Do not hide important navigation behind confusing interactions.

---

# 21. Images and Content Quality

Never use placeholder content unless explicitly requested.

Do not use:

- Lorem ipsum
- Fake testimonials
- Fake statistics
- Fake school names
- Fake client logos
- Fake awards
- Fake reviews
- Fake claims
- Placeholder images presented as real content

If content is unavailable:

- Use clearly identified placeholders only when necessary.
- Do not invent factual claims.
- Do not invent marketing proof.
- Do not fabricate social proof.

For real production assets, prioritize high-quality, relevant photography.

---

# 22. Content and Conversion UX

Content must support the user's decision-making process.

Important pages should clearly communicate:

- What the program is
- Who it is for
- Why it matters
- Benefits
- How it works
- Evidence/credibility where available
- What makes the program different
- What the next step is

Avoid unnecessary text merely to make pages longer.

Marketing content should be clear, credible, concise, and persuasive.

---

# 23. SEO-Friendly Content Structure

Where applicable, structure pages to support both users and search engines.

Use:

- Clear H1
- Logical H2/H3 hierarchy
- Descriptive headings
- Internal links
- Relevant supporting content
- FAQs where genuinely useful
- Structured data where appropriate

Do not create SEO content solely to manipulate rankings.

---

# 24. URLs and Routing

Routes must be:

- Readable
- Stable
- Descriptive
- SEO-friendly

Avoid unnecessary query parameters for permanent page identity.

Do not change an established production URL without considering redirects and SEO impact.

When replacing URLs, preserve SEO value through appropriate redirects where required.

---

# 25. Third-Party Services

Before adding a third-party package, SDK, widget, script, analytics provider, or external service:

- Confirm it is actually necessary.
- Check whether existing functionality can solve the problem.
- Consider performance impact.
- Consider privacy/security implications.
- Consider vendor lock-in.
- Consider whether it works with Vercel.
- Keep third-party integrations isolated where practical.

Never add dependencies merely because they are convenient.

---

# 26. Dependency Management

Before installing a package:

1. Check whether the project already has a suitable dependency.
2. Check whether native platform/framework functionality is sufficient.
3. Prefer mature, maintained dependencies.
4. Avoid dependencies that are excessive for the problem.
5. Avoid duplicate libraries solving the same problem.

Do not add packages without justification.

---

# 27. Testing and Verification

Every meaningful change must be verified.

Before considering a task complete:

- Run relevant tests.
- Run linting.
- Run type checking.
- Run the production build where appropriate.
- Check for runtime errors.
- Check affected pages.
- Check mobile responsiveness.
- Check desktop responsiveness.
- Check forms.
- Check links.
- Check CTAs.
- Check media.
- Check analytics/conversion events where applicable.
- Check SEO metadata for affected pages.

For significant UI changes, inspect the result visually rather than relying only on successful compilation.

---

# 28. Performance Verification

For significant page changes, verify where practical:

- Lighthouse performance
- Core Web Vitals
- Image loading
- Font loading
- JavaScript bundle impact
- Layout stability
- Mobile performance

A page is not considered complete merely because it renders without errors.

---

# 29. Browser Compatibility

The website should behave consistently across modern:

- Chrome
- Safari
- Edge
- Firefox

Pay particular attention to:

- Mobile Safari
- Android browsers
- Responsive layouts
- Media playback
- Form controls
- Navigation
- Sticky elements
- Viewport behaviour

Do not assume Chrome desktop behaviour represents all users.

---

# 30. Git and Changes

- Keep changes focused.
- Do not modify unrelated files.
- Do not commit generated junk.
- Do not commit secrets.
- Do not commit temporary debugging code.
- Remove console/debug statements that are not intentionally required.
- Keep commits logically coherent where commit creation is part of the workflow.

Never use broad destructive commands without understanding their impact.

---

# 31. Debugging

When fixing a bug:

1. Identify the root cause.
2. Fix the underlying issue rather than masking the symptom.
3. Avoid unrelated refactoring.
4. Re-test the affected functionality.
5. Check for regressions.

Do not introduce fragile hacks merely to make an error disappear.

---

# 32. Production Safety

Before production deployment, verify:

- Build succeeds.
- No secrets are exposed.
- No development URLs remain.
- No placeholder content remains unintentionally.
- SEO metadata is correct.
- Sitemap is correct.
- robots.txt is correct.
- Analytics is configured correctly.
- Forms work.
- CTAs work.
- Media loads correctly.
- Mobile layouts work.
- Desktop layouts work.
- Error states work.
- HTTPS works.
- Production environment variables are configured.
- No obvious console errors remain.
- No broken links remain.

---

# 33. Change Discipline

Before changing an existing feature:

- Understand how it currently works.
- Search for dependencies/usages.
- Determine whether the behaviour is shared.
- Avoid breaking existing consumers.
- Preserve existing contracts unless explicitly instructed otherwise.

Never rewrite an existing system simply because another implementation is aesthetically preferable.

---

# 34. Cursor Behaviour

When implementing a task:

- Inspect the existing codebase before creating new code.
- Reuse existing patterns whenever possible.
- Do not invent architecture unnecessarily.
- Do not generate duplicate components.
- Do not create unnecessary files.
- Do not modify unrelated files.
- Prefer reusable solutions.
- Think about mobile, SEO, accessibility, performance, security, and maintainability before implementation.
- Consider the production consequences of every change.
- Do not assume a feature is complete merely because it compiles.
- Verify the actual user experience.
- Flag any change that conflicts with this rule book before proceeding or immediately after discovering the conflict.

When uncertain between two technically valid approaches, prefer the one that:

1. Is simpler
2. Is more reusable
3. Is more performant
4. Is more accessible
5. Is easier to maintain
6. Is safer
7. Is more SEO-friendly

---

# 35. Definition of Done

A task is complete only when:

- The requested functionality works.
- Existing functionality remains intact.
- The implementation is responsive.
- The UI remains visually polished.
- Code is reusable.
- No unnecessary duplication exists.
- Performance has been considered.
- SEO has been considered.
- Accessibility has been considered.
- Security has been considered.
- Media works correctly.
- Error/loading states are handled where applicable.
- Relevant tests/lint/type checks/builds pass.
- No secrets are exposed.
- No unintended placeholder content remains.
- The result is suitable for production deployment.

---

